  <p class="label">About</p>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    I'm a platform-services engineer specialized in identity and authorization
    infrastructure, with distributed-systems depth. Every professional system I
    have shipped is a shared primitive consumed by other engineering teams:
    authentication services, authorization policy distribution, Kubernetes
    operators with CRDs as declarative APIs, a UID generation service. I'm not a
    product engineer; I build the layer product engineers build on.
  </p>

  <h2>Career arc</h2>
  <p>
    I studied computer science at NYU with minors in data science and
    mathematics, and the quantitative foundation still shows up in how I work:
    when I defend a design, I'd rather say "collision probability around
    10<sup>-18</sup>" than "unlikely." I joined FICO's platform organization as
    an intern in 2021, returned full time in 2022, and was promoted to Lead
    Software Engineer in 2026, all on the same identity and access management
    platform. Joining a brand-new platform initiative early meant I watched, and
    then increasingly shaped, systems growing from first gRPC APIs into
    infrastructure serving enterprise financial institutions under a 99.98%
    uptime SLA.
  </p>
  <p>
    The professional depth has deliberately narrowed: IAM, Kubernetes operators,
    distributed infrastructure. The breadth comes from side projects, which I
    use to explore what my day job doesn't cover: machine-learning pipelines in
    <a href="/projects/">StatStack</a>, LLM agents in Compass. Work stays in
    Java and infrastructure; side projects wander through Python, ML, and AI on
    purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>How I work</h2>
  <p>
    Some patterns recur often enough across my work that I trust them as
    descriptions rather than aspirations.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>I gravitate to zero-to-one infrastructure.</strong> Two Kubernetes
      operators, a UID service, a feature-flag module, and two complete side
      projects, all built from scratch. I prefer owning a service end to end
      (design, implementation, deployment, observability, on-call) over
      contributing features to someone else's.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>I think in trade-offs, not solutions.</strong> Every design
      decision names what it bought and what it cost. Stateless versus stateful,
      eventual versus strong consistency, availability versus correctness: the
      interesting engineering lives in what you knowingly gave up, which is why
      every project page on this site has a trade-offs section.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stateless by default.</strong> The UID service has no database,
      the authentication facade has no database, and authorization decisions
      run against locally cached policy. I add state only when a design forces
      it, because state is where availability and consistency problems live.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>I automate incident categories, not incidents.</strong> The best
      outcome I know is making a class of failure structurally impossible: the
      RBAC operator ended provisioning misconfigurations by making correct
      configuration the only expressible kind.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>I write things down.</strong> Design decisions, trade-off records,
      honest reflections on what I'd do differently. Infrastructure work is
      judged over years, and undocumented reasoning doesn't survive that long.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I'm looking for</h2>
  <p>
    Platform services, distributed systems, and infrastructure that other
    engineers build on: internal developer platforms, service meshes, policy
    engines, orchestration, AI infrastructure. I'm most useful on teams whose
    customers are other engineering teams, and I'm deliberately open to domains
    beyond identity. If that sounds like your team,
    <a href="mailto:saldanaj27@gmail.com">say hello</a>.
  </p>
